A3L3 = build(
    "s2-eng-a3-l3-nouns-and-noun-groups", "Nouns and Noun Groups",
    "Name things precisely, then build rich noun groups that paint a picture.",
    "Language: nouns and noun groups",
    ["EN2-CWT-01", "EN2-VOCAB-01", "EN2-RECOM-01"],
    {"EN2-CWT-01": "Primary. Uses nouns and expanded noun groups to add detail to written sentences.",
     "EN2-VOCAB-01": "Learns the terms noun, common noun, proper noun, collective noun, abstract noun, noun group and determiner.",
     "EN2-RECOM-01": "Identifies nouns and noun groups when reading to understand description."},
    "We are learning to identify different kinds of nouns and to build noun groups that add detail to our writing.",
    ["I can say what a noun is.", "I can tell common and proper nouns apart.", "I can name collective and abstract nouns.", "I can build a noun group with a determiner, adjectives and a noun.", "I can add detail after the noun."],
    ["noun", "common noun", "proper noun", "collective noun", "abstract noun", "noun group", "determiner"],
    ["Paper or notebook", "Pencil", "A page from a story you have read"],
    "Child can read simple sentences and knows what an adjective is in a basic way. Nouns and noun groups are taught from the beginning.",
    "A NOUN is a word that names a person, place, thing or idea. Nouns are the building blocks of sentences. Every sentence has at least one noun or a word that stands in for one.\n\nThere are different kinds of nouns. Some name one particular thing, such as Sydney. Some name any thing of a type, such as city. Some name a group, such as a flock. Some name ideas you cannot touch, such as courage.\n\nWriters also build NOUN GROUPS. A noun group is a noun with extra words that tell us more about it. 'A dog' becomes 'the scruffy brown dog with the wagging tail'. Noun groups are one of the best ways to make your writing detailed and vivid.",
    [
        _step("1", "What is a noun?",
              "A noun names a person, an animal, a place, a thing or an idea.\n\nPerson: teacher, Mum, astronaut.\nAnimal: kangaroo, spider.\nPlace: beach, library, Tasmania.\nThing: pencil, bicycle, cloud.\nIdea: freedom, joy.\n\nA trick for finding nouns: try putting 'the' in front. 'The pencil' works, so pencil is a noun. 'The quickly' does not, so quickly is not.\n\nTo find nouns in a sentence, ask: who or what is this sentence about? 'The cat chased the mouse around the kitchen.' The nouns are cat, mouse and kitchen.",
              "Sentence: Mia packed her bag and walked to school. Nouns: Mia, bag, school.",
              "Nouns name people, animals, places, things and ideas.",
              ("Which word is a noun?", ["river", "quickly", "jump"], 0, "A river is a thing.")),
        _step("2", "Common and proper nouns",
              "A COMMON NOUN names any person, place or thing of a general type. It does not start with a capital letter unless it begins a sentence. Examples: girl, city, river, school, dog.\n\nA PROPER NOUN names one particular person, place or thing. It always starts with a capital letter. Examples: Sophie, Melbourne, the Murray River, Bondi Primary School, Rex.\n\nThe same thing can have both. 'Dog' is common, and 'Rex' is the proper noun for one dog. 'River' is common, and 'Murray River' is proper.\n\nProper nouns include names of people and pets, places, days, months, holidays, languages and titles of books and films.",
              "Common to proper: city to Brisbane, day to Tuesday, girl to Ava, country to Australia.",
              "Proper nouns are specific names and have capital letters.",
              ("Which is a proper noun?", ["Canberra", "city", "capital"], 0, "Canberra names one particular place.")),
        _step("3", "Collective nouns",
              "A COLLECTIVE NOUN names a group of people, animals or things as one. We use it when we think of the group as a single unit.\n\nA team of players. A class of students. A flock of birds. A herd of cattle. A pack of wolves. A bunch of grapes. A pod of dolphins. A swarm of bees. A fleet of ships.\n\nCollective nouns make writing more precise and more interesting. 'A swarm of bees' creates a better picture than 'lots of bees'.\n\nTo remember them, try matching each to its group in a list and practise saying them aloud.",
              "Instead of 'many fish' write 'a school of fish'. Instead of 'many sheep' write 'a flock of sheep'.",
              "Collective nouns name a group as one.",
              ("Which is a collective noun?", ["flock", "bird", "fly"], 0, "A flock is a group of birds.")),
        _step("4", "Abstract nouns",
              "An ABSTRACT NOUN names an idea, feeling or quality that you cannot see, touch, hear, smell or taste. A concrete noun, like table or apple, is something you can sense.\n\nAbstract nouns: happiness, courage, friendship, kindness, hunger, danger, honesty, freedom, fear.\n\nA test: can I touch it? Can I take a photo of it? If not, it is likely abstract.\n\nMany abstract nouns come from other words. Brave becomes bravery. Kind becomes kindness. Happy becomes happiness. Strong becomes strength.\n\nWriters use abstract nouns to talk about feelings and themes. 'Her courage saved them.'",
              "Concrete: apple, tree. Abstract: hunger, fear. The test: you cannot touch fear.",
              "Abstract nouns name ideas, feelings and qualities.",
              ("Which is an abstract noun?", ["courage", "table", "puppy"], 0, "Courage is a quality, not a thing you can touch.")),
        _step("5", "What is a noun group?",
              "A NOUN GROUP is a noun plus the words that give more information about it. The noun is the HEAD, which is the main word. The other words add detail.\n\nSimple noun: dog.\nNoun group: the scruffy brown dog.\n\nIn this noun group, 'dog' is the head. 'The' is a determiner. 'Scruffy' and 'brown' are adjectives that describe it.\n\nA DETERMINER is a small word that comes before the noun and tells us which one or how many. Examples: a, an, the, this, that, my, your, some, three, many.\n\nPattern: determiner + adjectives + noun. 'My' + 'old, creaky' + 'wooden gate'. Notice that the adjectives often go in a natural order, such as opinion, size, age, colour, then material: 'a lovely, big, old, red brick house'.",
              "Build up: a dog. A brown dog. The scruffy brown dog. The scruffy brown dog with floppy ears.",
              "Noun group: determiner + adjectives + noun.",
              ("In 'the tall oak tree', which word is the head?", ["tree", "tall", "the"], 0, "Tree is the main noun.")),
        _step("6", "Add detail after the noun",
              "You can also add information AFTER the noun. This is often a group of words starting with a word like with, in, on, from, of or that.\n\nThe dog with the wagging tail.\nThe girl in the red coat.\nThe house on the hill.\nThe book that I borrowed.\nThe boy who lives next door.\n\nThese add information about which one we mean, so the reader can picture it exactly.\n\nThe full noun group can have words before and after the noun: 'the scruffy brown dog with the wagging tail'.\n\nBe careful not to pile on too much. Two or three details are usually enough. Choose the details that matter most to your story.",
              "Simple: The fox ran. Detail: The small red fox with the floppy ear ran.",
              "Add words before the noun and after it for detail.",
              ("Which adds detail AFTER the noun?", ["with the wagging tail", "the scruffy", "very quickly"], 0, "With the wagging tail follows the noun.")),
        _step("7", "Use noun groups in your writing",
              "Now use noun groups to improve your own sentences. Follow these steps.\n\n1. Choose a plain sentence, such as 'The boy found a box.'\n2. Find the nouns: boy and box.\n3. Expand each into a noun group. 'The nervous young boy found a dusty wooden box with a rusty lock.'\n4. Read it aloud. Does it sound natural? Does it paint a clear picture?\n5. Check that the detail is useful. Cut anything that does not help.\n\nVary your noun groups. Not every noun needs to be long. A short noun can have a big effect after several long ones.\n\nWhen you read, look for noun groups the author uses. Notice how they help you picture the scene.",
              "Plain: The woman opened the door. Better: The tired old woman opened the creaky front door with a trembling hand.",
              "Expand nouns into noun groups, then check it sounds natural.",
              ("Why use noun groups?", ["To add clear detail", "To make sentences shorter", "To avoid nouns"], 0, "They add detail and help the reader picture things.")),
    ],
    "Model answers. Nouns in 'The teacher read a book about dolphins in the library': teacher, book, dolphins, library. Common/proper: Sydney is proper; city is common. Collective: a pod of dolphins. Abstract: kindness. Noun group: the shy young girl with a red backpack (determiner the, adjectives shy young, head noun girl, detail with a red backpack). Improved sentence: The tiny silver fish darted between the rocks.",
    "1) Find the nouns in five sentences. 2) Sort ten nouns into common and proper and add capital letters. 3) Match five collective nouns to their groups. 4) Write three abstract nouns and use each in a sentence. 5) Build a noun group for each of five plain nouns. 6) Rewrite three plain sentences using noun groups.",
    "Rewrite five plain sentences using noun groups with words before and after the noun. Underline the head noun in each. Upload a photo of your writing.",
    CHECK,
    "Did I use a determiner in each noun group? Did I add at least one adjective before the noun? Did I add detail after the noun in at least two? Are proper nouns capitalised? Do my sentences sound natural? Did I underline the head noun?",
    [
        _q("What is a noun?", ["A word that names a person, place, thing or idea", "An action word", "A describing word"], 0, "Nouns are naming words."),
        _q("Which is a proper noun?", ["Tasmania", "island", "state"], 0, "Tasmania names one particular place."),
        _q("Which is a collective noun?", ["pod", "whale", "swim"], 0, "A pod is a group of whales or dolphins."),
        _q("Which is an abstract noun?", ["friendship", "bench", "pencil"], 0, "Friendship is an idea."),
        _q("Which word is a determiner?", ["the", "happy", "jump"], 0, "The tells us which noun."),
        _q("In 'the old wooden gate', the head noun is...", ["gate", "old", "wooden"], 0, "The head is the main noun."),
        _q("Which is a noun group?", ["the shy young girl with a red backpack", "ran quickly", "very tall"], 0, "It has a head noun with extra detail."),
        _q("Proper nouns always begin with...", ["a capital letter", "a comma", "a number"], 0, "Proper nouns are capitalised."),
    ],
    "Upload a photo of your noun group sentences.",
    "Find a paragraph in a book of your choice and copy three noun groups the author used. Say what each one helps you picture.",
    [("noun", "A word that names a person, animal, place, thing or idea."), ("common noun", "A general name for a type of person, place or thing."), ("proper noun", "The specific name of a person, place or thing, written with a capital letter."),
     ("collective noun", "A noun that names a group, such as a flock."), ("abstract noun", "A noun for an idea or feeling you cannot touch."), ("noun group", "A noun with extra words that give more detail."), ("determiner", "A small word such as the, a or my that comes before a noun.")],
    [
        _video("Common and Proper Nouns for Kids", "gQsZr8yrsno",
               "Watch how common nouns name a type of person, place or thing, and proper nouns name a specific one with a capital letter. Notice the examples like dog and Fido.",
               "Think of a common noun and a proper noun for it.",
               ("What do proper nouns always begin with?", ["A capital letter", "A comma", "A small letter"], 0, "Proper nouns always have a capital letter.")),
    ],
    _sort("Noun sorter", "What kind of noun is each word? Tap a kind, then Check.",
          ["Common", "Proper", "Collective", "Abstract"],
          [("river", 0), ("teacher", 0), ("Melbourne", 1), ("Monday", 1), ("flock", 2), ("team", 2), ("courage", 3), ("happiness", 3)]),
    [
        _wc("A word that names a person, place or thing is a ___.", ["noun", "verb", "adverb"], 0, "Nouns are naming words."),
        _wc("A group of dolphins is called a ___ of dolphins.", ["pod", "herd", "bunch"], 0, "A pod is a group of dolphins."),
        _wc("The word 'the' is a ___.", ["determiner", "noun", "verb"], 0, "It comes before a noun."),
        _wc("Which is spelt correctly?", ["collective", "colective", "collectif"], 0, "Collective has two l letters."),
        _wc("Which is spelt correctly?", ["adjective", "adjecktive", "adgective"], 0, "Adjective has a j and ective."),
    ],
    [
        {"key": "nouns", "label": "Nouns I found", "hint": "Nouns from five sentences."},
        {"key": "kinds", "label": "Kinds of nouns", "hint": "Common, proper, collective and abstract examples."},
        {"key": "groups", "label": "My noun groups", "hint": "Five noun groups with the head noun underlined."},
        {"key": "sentences", "label": "Improved sentences", "hint": "Three plain sentences rewritten with noun groups."},
    ],
    ["Forgetting capital letters on proper nouns.", "Using too many adjectives so the noun group becomes clumsy.", "Confusing the head noun with an adjective.", "Thinking a collective noun is always plural.", "Leaving out the determiner."],
    [{"title": "Noun Group Hunt", "description": "Find five noun groups in a book you are reading and rewrite each one by changing the adjectives.", "type": "write", "difficulty": "medium", "evidence_type": "photo"}],
    "Answers vary. Check that nouns are correctly identified, proper nouns have capital letters, noun groups have a determiner, one or two adjectives and a head noun, some have detail after the noun, and sentences sound natural. Sorter: Common: river, teacher. Proper: Melbourne, Monday. Collective: flock, team. Abstract: courage, happiness.",
)

A3L4 = build(
    "s2-eng-a3-l4-suffixes-ful-less-ness", "Spelling: Suffixes -ful, -less and -ness",
    "Add endings to words and learn the spelling rules that keep them correct.",
    "Spelling: suffixes",
    ["EN2-SPELL-01", "EN2-VOCAB-01", "EN2-CWT-01"],
    {"EN2-SPELL-01": "Primary. Applies spelling rules when adding the suffixes -ful, -less and -ness to base words.",
     "EN2-VOCAB-01": "Learns the terms base word, suffix, root and how suffixes change meaning and word class.",
     "EN2-CWT-01": "Uses suffixed words accurately in written sentences."},
    "We are learning how the suffixes -ful, -less and -ness change a word, and how to spell the new words correctly.",
    ["I can say what a base word and a suffix are.", "I can explain what -ful, -less and -ness mean.", "I can spell -ful with one l.", "I can change y to i before adding a suffix.", "I can spell and use words with these suffixes."],
    ["base word", "suffix", "-ful", "-less", "-ness", "noun", "adjective"],
    ["Paper or notebook", "Pencil", "Coloured pencils"],
    "Child can spell common words and knows what a noun and an adjective are in a simple way. Suffix rules are taught from the beginning.",
    "A BASE WORD is a word that can stand on its own, such as care, help or kind. A SUFFIX is a group of letters added to the END of a base word to change its meaning or job.\n\nSuffixes are powerful. One small ending can turn a noun into an adjective or an adjective into a noun. Learn a few suffixes and you can spell and understand hundreds of words.\n\nIn this lesson you will meet three suffixes: -ful, -less and -ness. You will learn what each means, and the spelling rules for adding them.",
    [
        _step("1", "Base words and suffixes",
              "A base word has a meaning on its own. Examples: care, help, kind, hope, joy.\n\nA suffix is added at the end. It changes the meaning and often the type of word.\n\ncare + ful = careful (an adjective)\nhelp + less = helpless (an adjective)\nkind + ness = kindness (a noun)\n\nWhen you meet a long word, look for the base word first, then the suffix. That makes it easier to read, to spell and to work out what it means.\n\nTo spell: say the base word, then say the suffix, then put them together.",
              "Split: thankful = thank + ful. Hopeless = hope + less. Darkness = dark + ness.",
              "Base word plus suffix makes a new word.",
              ("What is a suffix?", ["Letters added to the end of a word", "Letters at the start", "A whole sentence"], 0, "Suffixes come at the end.")),
        _step("2", "The suffix -ful",
              "The suffix -ful means FULL OF. Care + ful means full of care. Joy + ful means full of joy.\n\nAdding -ful usually turns a noun into an adjective, which is a describing word.\n\ncolour + ful = colourful\nhelp + ful = helpful\nuse + ful = useful\nthank + ful = thankful\nhope + ful = hopeful\npain + ful = painful\n\nIMPORTANT: the suffix -ful has only ONE l. It is not spelt full. Remember: a cupful is not very full, so we drop one l.\n\nAustralian spelling keeps the u in colour, so we write colourful.",
              "Check: careful (not carefull), joyful (not joyfull), wonderful (not wonderfull).",
              "-ful means full of, and has one l.",
              ("Which is spelt correctly?", ["helpful", "helpfull", "helpfal"], 0, "The suffix -ful has one l.")),
        _step("3", "The suffix -less",
              "The suffix -less means WITHOUT. Care + less means without care. Hope + less means without hope.\n\nIt also turns a noun into an adjective, and it is the opposite of -ful.\n\ncareful and careless\nhelpful and helpless\nhopeful and hopeless\nuseful and useless\nthankful and thankless\n\nOther words: fearless, endless, harmless, painless, homeless, speechless, breathless.\n\nIn -less there are two s letters. Notice that the base word does not change in most cases, so you just join the two parts.\n\nBe careful: some words ending in -less are not made from a base word you know, such as 'unless' and 'bless'. Focus on the ones that are.",
              "Pairs: fear + less = fearless (without fear). Home + less = homeless (without a home).",
              "-less means without, and has two s letters.",
              ("What does -less mean?", ["Without", "Full of", "Again"], 0, "-less means without.")),
        _step("4", "The suffix -ness",
              "The suffix -ness turns an adjective into a NOUN. It names the state or quality of being something.\n\nkind + ness = kindness (the quality of being kind)\ndark + ness = darkness\nsad + ness = sadness\nill + ness = illness\nsweet + ness = sweetness\n\nWhen the base word already ends in n, keep it and add -ness. This gives two n letters: mean + ness = meanness, clean + ness = cleanness, open + ness = openness.\n\nWords made with -ness are abstract nouns. They name an idea or feeling, such as happiness, weakness or fairness.\n\nRemember: -ness has two s letters at the end.",
              "Adjective to noun: 'The room was dark.' becomes 'The darkness frightened her.' Dark is an adjective, darkness is a noun.",
              "-ness turns an adjective into a noun.",
              ("Which word is a noun?", ["kindness", "kind", "kindly"], 0, "The suffix -ness makes a noun.")),
        _step("5", "The y to i rule",
              "When a base word ends in a CONSONANT followed by y, change the y to i before adding the suffix.\n\nbeauty + ful = beautiful\nmercy + ful = merciful\nplenty + ful = plentiful\nhappy + ness = happiness\nlazy + ness = laziness\nbusy + ness = business\nworry + less = worryless (rare)\n\nIf the y comes after a VOWEL (a, e, i, o, u), keep the y.\njoy + ful = joyful\nplay + ful = playful\nenjoy + ment = enjoyment\n\nThe test: look at the letter before y. If it is a consonant, change y to i. If it is a vowel, leave it.",
              "Beauty: the letter before y is t, a consonant, so beautiful. Joy: the letter before y is o, a vowel, so joyful.",
              "Consonant then y: change y to i. Vowel then y: keep the y.",
              ("Which is spelt correctly?", ["happiness", "happyness", "hapiness"], 0, "Change y to i before -ness.")),
        _step("6", "Silent e and tricky words",
              "With -ful and -less, you keep the silent e on the base word: care + ful = careful, hope + less = hopeless. Do not drop it.\n\nWith -ness there is nothing to drop, so just add it: polite + ness = politeness.\n\nSome words drop the silent e when a suffix starting with a vowel is added, such as -ing or -ed, but -ful, -less and -ness start with consonants, so the e stays.\n\nOther tricky words to practise: wonderful, beautiful, successful, thoughtful, truthful, thoughtless, restless, weakness, fairness, forgiveness.\n\nPractise using Look, Say, Cover, Write, Check. Look carefully at the word and say it aloud. Cover it, write it from memory, then check it letter by letter.",
              "Keep the e: care becomes careful, use becomes useful, hope becomes hopeless.",
              "Keep silent e with -ful, -less and -ness.",
              ("What happens to the silent e in care + ful?", ["It stays", "It is dropped", "It becomes i"], 0, "-ful starts with a consonant, so the e stays.")),
        _step("7", "Use suffixed words in sentences",
              "Now use your words in writing. Follow these steps.\n\n1. Choose a base word, such as help.\n2. Add each suffix that works: helpful, helpless.\n3. Decide what each word means.\n4. Write a sentence for each, and check the meaning is right.\n\nExample: 'The helpful neighbour carried the shopping.' 'The kitten was helpless in the tree.'\n\nSuffixes help your writing too. Use -ful words to describe things positively and -less words to show something is missing. Use -ness words to name feelings: 'She felt a great sadness.'\n\nProofread your sentences. Check one l in -ful, two s letters in -less and -ness, and changed y to i where needed.",
              "Sentences: The beautiful sunset filled the sky. The hopeless puppy was lost. Her kindness made everyone smile.",
              "Choose the right suffix, then proofread the spelling.",
              ("Which word means 'without fear'?", ["fearless", "fearful", "fearness"], 0, "-less means without.")),
    ],
    "Model answers. careful = care + ful (full of care). helpless = help + less (without help). kindness = kind + ness (the quality of being kind). beautiful: beauty has a consonant before y, so change y to i. happiness: happy changes y to i. joyful: joy has a vowel before y, so keep y. Sentences: The careful boy carried the cup. The hopeless dog sat alone. Her kindness helped us.",
    "1) Split ten words into base word and suffix. 2) Add -ful to five nouns. 3) Add -less to five nouns. 4) Add -ness to five adjectives. 5) Sort words that need y changed to i from those that keep y. 6) Write six sentences, using two words from each suffix.",
    "Write eight sentences using words with -ful, -less and -ness, then underline the suffix in each. Upload a photo of your writing.",
    CHECK,
    "Does every -ful word have one l? Does every -less and -ness word have two s letters? Did I change y to i where the letter before y is a consonant? Did I keep the silent e? Do my sentences make sense? Did I underline each suffix?",
    [
        _q("What does -ful mean?", ["Full of", "Without", "Again"], 0, "-ful means full of."),
        _q("What does -less mean?", ["Without", "Full of", "More"], 0, "-less means without."),
        _q("Which is spelt correctly?", ["careful", "carefull", "carful"], 0, "-ful has one l."),
        _q("Which is spelt correctly?", ["happiness", "happyness", "hapiness"], 0, "Change y to i before -ness."),
        _q("Which is spelt correctly?", ["joyful", "joiful", "joyfull"], 0, "Keep y after a vowel."),
        _q("Which word is a noun?", ["sadness", "sad", "sadly"], 0, "-ness makes a noun."),
        _q("Which means 'without hope'?", ["hopeless", "hopeful", "hopeness"], 0, "-less means without."),
        _q("Which is spelt correctly?", ["beautiful", "beautyful", "beutiful"], 0, "Beauty changes y to i."),
    ],
    "Upload a photo of your suffix sentences.",
    "Find five other words ending in -ful, -less or -ness in a book of your choice. Write the base word and the meaning of each.",
    [("base word", "A word that can stand on its own."), ("suffix", "Letters added to the end of a word to change its meaning."), ("-ful", "A suffix meaning full of."),
     ("-less", "A suffix meaning without."), ("-ness", "A suffix that makes a noun showing a state or quality."), ("noun", "A word that names a person, place, thing or idea."), ("adjective", "A word that describes a noun.")],
    [
        _video("Spelling Secrets: What Are Suffixes?", "fgQ7jurdxF4",
               "Watch how suffixes are added to the end of base words to change their meaning, for example -ed changes walk to walked. Notice that every suffix carries its own meaning.",
               "Choose a base word and say what it becomes with -ful, -less and -ness.",
               ("Where does a suffix go?", ["At the end of a word", "At the start", "In the middle"], 0, "A suffix is added to the end of a word.")),
    ],
    _sort("Suffix sorter", "Which suffix goes with each meaning or word? Tap a suffix, then Check.",
          ["-ful", "-less", "-ness"],
          [("full of care", 0), ("full of joy", 0), ("without fear", 1), ("without a home", 1), ("the quality of being kind", 2), ("the state of being dark", 2)]),
    [
        _wc("The suffix meaning full of is ___.", ["-ful", "-less", "-ness"], 0, "-ful means full of."),
        _wc("The suffix meaning without is ___.", ["-less", "-ful", "-ness"], 0, "-less means without."),
        _wc("Which is spelt correctly?", ["kindness", "kindnes", "kindnass"], 0, "-ness has two s letters."),
        _wc("Which is spelt correctly?", ["harmless", "harmles", "harmlass"], 0, "-less has two s letters."),
        _wc("Which is spelt correctly?", ["colourful", "colourfull", "colorfull"], 0, "Australian spelling keeps u, and -ful has one l."),
    ],
    [
        {"key": "split", "label": "Splitting words", "hint": "Ten words split into base word and suffix."},
        {"key": "adding", "label": "Adding suffixes", "hint": "New words with -ful, -less and -ness."},
        {"key": "rule", "label": "The y to i rule", "hint": "Words where you changed y to i, and words where you kept y."},
        {"key": "sentences", "label": "My sentences", "hint": "Eight sentences with the suffix underlined."},
    ],
    ["Spelling -ful as full.", "Forgetting to change y to i, such as happyness.", "Dropping the silent e, such as carful.", "Writing only one s in -less or -ness.", "Mixing up -ful and -less."],
    [{"title": "Suffix Word Web", "description": "Choose five base words and make a word web showing every word you can build with -ful, -less and -ness.", "type": "write", "difficulty": "medium", "evidence_type": "photo"}],
    "Answers vary. Check that -ful has one l, -less and -ness have two s letters, y is changed to i after a consonant, the silent e is kept, and sentences use the words with correct meaning. Sorter: -ful: full of care, full of joy. -less: without fear, without a home. -ness: the quality of being kind, the state of being dark.",
)

